WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.596
Your ring finger has a vein that

00:00:01.606 --> 00:00:03.179
runs straight to your heart.

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:03.804
Myth.

00:00:03.814 --> 00:00:05.666
Finger veins drain into the veins of

00:00:05.676 --> 00:00:07.914
your hand, then up your arm.

00:00:07.951 --> 00:00:09.757
None goes straight to the heart.

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:11.247
Where did it start?

00:00:11.257 --> 00:00:13.929
A real Roman gold ring, about 1,800

00:00:13.939 --> 00:00:14.883
years old.

00:00:14.989 --> 00:00:17.454
In the second century, the Roman writer

00:00:17.464 --> 00:00:20.864
Aulus Gellius noted
where Romans wore rings:

00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:23.447
left hand, next to the little finger.

00:00:23.457 --> 00:00:24.057
Why?

00:00:24.069 --> 00:00:26.709
Egyptian dissections,
he wrote, had found a

00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:28.397
fine nerve from that finger to the

00:00:28.407 --> 00:00:29.191
heart.

00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:29.891
Notice:

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.381
a nerve, not a vein.

00:00:31.391 --> 00:00:34.415
The vein came later, in medieval writers.

00:00:34.425 --> 00:00:35.690
Here's the true part:

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.565
the custom really is Roman.

00:00:37.575 --> 00:00:39.478
The anatomy never was.

00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:41.438
So next time someone tells you...
